.TH PROGRAMMERS\-SCREENSHOT 1 "2026-09-03" "programmers-screenshot 0.26.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
programmers\-screenshot \- region screenshot tool for hotkey binding
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B programmers\-screenshot
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Freezes the screen and dims it, then puts a toolbar across the top of every
monitor. Mark out a region, draw on it, and press
.B Capture
in the top right corner. The result is copied to the clipboard and written as
a timestamped PNG to
.IR ~/Pictures/Screenshots ,
whose path is printed to standard output.
.PP
Nothing is captured until you say so, so a selection can be redrawn as many
times as you like first.
.SH SOUND
A shutter sound plays as the shot is taken, using the first of
.BR canberra\-gtk\-play (1),
.BR paplay (1),
.BR pw\-play (1)
or
.BR aplay (1)
that is installed. With none of them the capture is silent and nothing else
changes. The desktop's own event-sounds setting is honoured, and
.B \-\-no\-sound
suppresses it for one invocation.
.SH NOTIFICATION
The notification shown afterwards carries two buttons:
.TP
.B Open Image
Opens the PNG in whatever handles images.
.TP
.B Show in Folder
Opens the containing folder with the file selected, via the
.B org.freedesktop.FileManager1
interface. Without a file manager on the bus it falls back to opening the
folder itself.
.PP
GNOME collapses notifications that carry buttons, so the two may sit behind
the notification's expander arrow rather than being visible immediately.
.PP
Buttons need a process alive to receive the click, but the command itself
exits at once so shell pipelines do not block. The notification is therefore
owned by a short-lived detached helper, which exits when the notification is
dismissed, when a button is pressed, or after five minutes.
.SH TOOLS
.TP
.B Region
Click and drag to mark out what will be captured. Starting a new drag deletes
the region already there, and a single click clears it. The region is optional: with none marked out, Capture
takes the whole screen.
.TP
.B Pen
Draw freehand on the frozen screen, in the colour and thickness chosen on the
settings row. Annotations are baked into the captured image.
.TP
.B Highlighter
Freehand, like the pen, but laid down as a translucent wash in its own marker
colours. It tints what is underneath rather than covering it, so text stays
readable. Translucent rather than multiplied: multiply is what a highlighter
does on paper and looks better on a light screenshot, but it barely touches a
dark one, and most screenshots are dark.
.TP
.B Line
Drag out a straight line, a rectangle, an outlined circle, or an arrow,
whichever is chosen on the settings row. The rectangle and the circle are
outlines, so neither hides what it is drawn around.
.TP
.B Measure
Drag between two points to read the distance. The reading is in physical
pixels, which on a HiDPI screen is not the same as the logical ones the drag
covers. A drag along an axis reads as a single number; anything else shows
width, height and the diagonal. Holding Shift snaps to 45 degrees, which is
how you get an exactly horizontal or vertical reading. The measurement is
committed like any other annotation, so it ends up in the capture.
.TP
.B Colour picker
Click a pixel and its colour goes on the clipboard, as
.B #RRGGBB
or as
.BR rgb() ,
whichever the settings row says. A swatch and the value are shown where you
clicked so you can see that it worked. Nothing is added to the drawing, so
there is nothing to undo and nothing of it in the capture. Capturing puts the
image on the clipboard and so replaces the colour: press Escape instead if the
colour is what you came for.
.TP
.B Redact
Drag out a bar that covers something completely, in black or white. The fill
replaces the pixels underneath, so nothing of the original survives into the
saved PNG. This matters: pixelating or blurring a secret only averages the
pixels, and those averages leak \- pixelated text can be recovered by
rendering candidate strings, pixelating them the same way and matching. Use
this for tokens, keys and addresses.
.TP
.B Pixelate
Drag out an area and it is broken into coarse blocks, at one of three sizes.
The blocks are averaged from the frozen screen when the drag ends. This is for
faces and clutter, not for secrets: the averages leak, and pixelated text can
be recovered by rendering candidate strings, pixelating them the same way and
matching. Use
.B Redact
for anything that must not get out.
.TP
.B Step
Click to drop a numbered badge, counting 1, 2, 3 and on as you go, for marking
out the steps of a process. Drag before releasing to place one exactly.
Undoing a badge hands its number back, so the next one takes it again. The
numeral is drawn light or dark, whichever reads against the badge colour.
.TP
.B Text
Click to place a caret and type. Enter starts a new line, Backspace deletes,
and clicking away commits the text. Switching tools or capturing commits it
too, so nothing typed is lost. With
.B Backing
turned on the text sits on a filled white box, one box behind the whole
paragraph, as wide as the longest line and as tall as all of them together.
The box is always white; only the text takes the chosen colour.
.PP
Holding
.B Shift
while dragging constrains it: the region, the rectangle and the circle go
square, and lines and arrows snap to 45 degree angles.
.PP
A tool that has settings gets a second toolbar row beneath the first, holding
its own options. Setting values are shared between tools, so a colour chosen
for one is the colour the next one uses.
.PP
Hovering any button on either row names it, which is the only place the
colour swatches and the line tool's shape icons are labelled.
.SH INTERFACE
.TP
.B Floating toolbar
Off by default. Turn it on in the settings window and the controls become one
compact rectangle instead of a bar across every screen. Drag it by the ribbed
strip along its top; it remembers where you left it, and can be dragged to any
monitor. It cannot be dragged so far that the strip leaves every screen.
.TP
.B Sub-tools
Related tools share a button, and a tool that draws more than one thing wears
a small wedge in the corner of its button. Click the wedge for a flyout of the alternatives \- the line tool
offers a line, a rectangle, a circle and an arrow that way, the pen shares
with the highlighter, and Black Bar shares with Pixelate. The button itself
shows whichever is chosen, so it says what it will draw, and remembers it, so
picking the same one again is a single click. Escape or a click elsewhere
closes the flyout.
.TP
.B Eraser
Drag across the canvas to rub out the parts of any mark you sweep through:
half a pen stroke, a bite out of a rectangle, a hole in a pixelate block. A
plain click with no drag removes the whole mark instead. Marks open up under
the pointer as you drag rather than when you release. The holes are kept
as geometry rather than as a bitmap, so they come out as sharp in the saved
PNG as they look on screen. One drag undoes as one step however many marks it
touched. Clicking a numbered step closes the gap, so the badges after it count
down and the next one you place carries on the sequence.
.IP
Rubbing out part of a redaction bar uncovers what was under it. That is what
you asked for, but it is worth knowing before you do it to something you were
hiding on purpose.
.TP
.B Capture
Top right. Always available.
.TP
.B ✕
Left of Capture. Closes without capturing.
.TP
.B Sliders
Left of ✕. Opens the settings window, which chooses whether captures are
saved to a folder and which folder that is. What it stores is a default: an
explicit
.B \-\-directory
or
.B \-\-no\-save
on the command line still wins.
.TP
.B Enter
Same as pressing Capture.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Z ", " Ctrl+Shift+Z
Undo and redo. Setting the region is undoable just as drawing is.
.TP
.B Escape ", right click"
Cancel. Nothing is written and the exit status is 1. Pressing Escape during a
drag abandons only that stroke, and while typing it discards only the text.
.PP
The toolbar is not a drawing surface, so a drag cannot be *started* underneath
it. Dragging upwards from below into the toolbar strip does still work, which
is how you capture the top edge of the screen.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-full
Capture the entire screen immediately, skipping the overlay entirely.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Write the PNG to \fIFILE\fR instead of a timestamped name.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-directory " " \fIDIR\fR
Save into \fIDIR\fR instead of the default directory, overriding the folder
chosen in the settings window.
.TP
.BR \-\-window " " \fITITLE\fR
Capture the window whose title contains \fITITLE\fR, whatever is stacked on
top of it. Nothing is raised or focused: under a compositor each window is
drawn to an offscreen pixmap of its own, so a completely buried window can be
photographed without disturbing it, and nothing lying over it gets into the
picture. Coordinates are then relative to that window's own top left corner.
The title must pick out one window; if it matches several, the message lists
them. X11 only, and needs libwnck (\fBgir1.2\-wnck\-3.0\fR). Off until
enabled; see
.BR RECIPES .
.TP
.BR \-\-input " " \fIFILE\fR
Annotate \fIFILE\fR instead of capturing anything. Coordinates are then
measured from the top left of that picture.
.IP
This is how a browser \fItab\fR gets annotated. Nothing outside a browser can
address a tab: a tab is not a window, and only the front tab of a window is
being drawn at all, so a background tab has no pixels on the screen to
photograph. Naming a window, or having a page mark itself somehow, is only ever
a guess about which tab is in front, and it is wrong the moment somebody
switches tabs. A browser can capture the tab it means; let it, and annotate
what it produces. Quiet: no shutter and no notification, because no screen was
read.
.TP
.BR \-\-scale " " \fIFACTOR\fR
The picture holds this many of its own pixels per one of yours. A browser
screenshot 1242 pixels wide of a 1720 pixel viewport is \fB\-\-scale
0.7221\fR, and then every coordinate can be the page's own, straight out of
.BR getBoundingClientRect() .
Positions and sizes are scaled; how a mark is drawn is not, so a width of 4 is
4 pixels of the picture either way.
.TP
.BR \-\-viewport " " \fIWIDTH\fR
The picture shows a page this many logical pixels wide \-
.B window.innerWidth
\- and the scale follows from the picture's own width, so nothing has to open
the file to measure it.
.B \-\-scale
wins if both are given.
.TP
.BR \-\-origin " " \fIX,Y\fR
Measure every coordinate from that point instead of from the corner of what
was captured.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-windows
Print the windows
.B \-\-window
can name, with their sizes and positions, and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-region " " \fIX,Y,W,H\fR
Capture that area and nothing else, without ever showing the overlay.
Coordinates are logical pixels from the top left of whatever is being
captured: the named window with
.BR \-\-window ,
or the whole virtual screen without it. Off until enabled; see
.BR RECIPES .
.TP
.BR \-\-delay " " \fISECONDS\fR
Wait before the screen is read, so a menu or a hover state can be set up
first. Works with the overlay too: the delay happens before the screen is
frozen, so what you arrange during it is what you get to draw on.
.TP
.BR \-\-recipe " " \fIFILE\fR
Take the shot described by a JSON recipe, with no overlay and nobody at the
keyboard. \fB\-\fR reads it from standard input. See
.BR RECIPES .
.TP
.B \-\-recipe\-help
Print the whole of what a recipe can describe \- every mark, every parameter,
the coordinate frame and a worked example \- and exit. This is the reference,
generated from the parser's own table, and it is what to read (or hand to a
program) before writing one. Needs no display.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-clipboard
Do not touch the clipboard.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-save
Do not write a file; copy to the clipboard only. The settings window can
make this the default.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-sound
Do not play the shutter sound.
.TP
.BR \-\-install\-hotkey " [" \fIACCEL\fR ]
Register a GNOME custom shortcut that runs this command. \fIACCEL\fR is a
GTK accelerator string and defaults to \fBPrint\fR.
.IP
GNOME holds Print for its own screenshot UI, and a custom shortcut does not
reliably win while it does, so this switches the built-in off and reports
what it took. The old value is written to
.I ~/.config/programmers-screenshot/displaced.json
and
.B \-\-uninstall\-hotkey
puts it back. An accelerator nobody holds disturbs nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-uninstall\-hotkey
Remove the shortcut registered by \fB\-\-install\-hotkey\fR, and restore any
GNOME binding it displaced.
.TP
.B \-\-install\-skill
Write a Claude Code skill to
.IR ~/.claude/skills/programmers\-screenshot/SKILL.md ,
so that a session in any project knows this command exists and how to drive
it. Deliberately thin: it says what the three ways in are, how a browser tab
is done, and to run
.BR \-\-recipe\-help ,
which is generated and so cannot go stale. Writing it does not switch recipes
on \- that is still a decision made in the settings window.
.TP
.B \-\-uninstall\-skill
Remove that file, and the directory it sits in if nothing else was put there.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
A screenshot was captured and delivered.
.TP
.B 1
The capture was cancelled, or the screen could not be read.
.TP
.B 2
The options given were contradictory, or a recipe could not be understood \-
in which case nothing was captured and nothing was drawn.
.SH RECIPES
A screenshot can be described rather than taken by hand, so that something
other than a person can produce one: a window or a picture to annotate, an
origin and a scale or viewport width, a region, a delay, an output path and a
list of marks, as JSON.
.PP
.EX
{"region": [100, 100, 900, 500],
 "annotate": [{"box": [150, 150, 350, 150], "colour": "red"},
              {"arrow": [[650, 420], [520, 310]]},
              {"step": [200, 200]},
              {"redact": [160, 330, 300, 40]}]}
.EE
.PP
A box, an ellipse, a line, an arrow, a numbered step, a label, a redaction
bar and a pixelated patch are all expressible, in the same colours and sizes
the toolbar offers. Coordinates are logical pixels from the top left of what
is being captured \- the named window, or the whole virtual screen \- and
that goes for the marks as well as the region, and
.B \-\-origin
and
.B \-\-scale
shift and stretch that frame when the numbers to hand are in another one.
Nothing is ever relative to the region.
.PP
.B \-\-recipe\-help
prints the full reference, including how to map coordinates out of a browser
onto the screen. Nothing is drawn until the whole recipe has been understood,
so a mistake in the last arrow does not leave the first three baked into a
half-finished image; the message names the entry, and the exit status is 2.
.PP
Off until switched on, in the settings window, for the same reason the update
check is: a person pressing Print Screen knows what is on their screen, and
something running a recipe does not. It may catch a password manager, a
private message or a token in a terminal, and then write it to a file.
Prefer a named
.B \-\-region
to the whole screen, and redact anything sensitive in the same recipe \- a
redaction replaces the pixels, so nothing of the original reaches the PNG.
The shutter still sounds, which is how the person at the desk knows.
.PP
It reads the real screen, so it needs a live desktop session on the same
machine. Over plain ssh, in a container or in CI there is no display and it
exits 1.
.SH UPDATES
Off by default, and the only time the program touches the network. Switch it
on in the settings window and it asks github.com once a day, after a capture
has finished, in a process of its own \- never between the keypress and the
overlay. If a newer release exists you get a notification with a button to the
release notes. It is a window rather than a desktop notification, so it waits
to be dismissed instead of vanishing while you are looking elsewhere. It will
not download or install anything: this is a .deb, and apt is what installs it.
.PP
Nothing is sent, and nothing is said when there is nothing to say. Being up to
date, being offline, being rate limited and getting a reply that does not
parse are all silent.
.PP
Separately, and with no network involved, the first run after an upgrade
reports what changed in a window, read from the changelog shipped inside the
package. Not on a first install, not on a downgrade, and not twice.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/Pictures/Screenshots
Default output directory, created on first use.
.TP
.I ~/.config/programmers-screenshot/preferences.json
What the settings window stores.
.TP
.I ~/.config/programmers-screenshot/state.json
Bookkeeping the program keeps for itself: when it last asked about releases,
which version it last mentioned, which version last ran. Kept apart from your
settings so neither has to be edited around the other.
.TP
.I ~/.config/programmers-screenshot/displaced.json
GNOME shortcuts switched off to free the hotkey, kept so they can be restored.
.TP
.I ~/.claude/skills/programmers-screenshot/SKILL.md
Written by \fB\-\-install\-skill\fR, and removed by \fB\-\-uninstall\-skill\fR.
.SH NOTES
Under X11 the screen is captured directly from the root window. Under Wayland
it goes through the
.B org.gnome.Shell.Screenshot
D\-Bus interface, so a GNOME session is required there.
.PP
The overlay spans every monitor, so a selection may cross screen boundaries.
Every monitor gets its own copy of the toolbar, laid out to that screen's
width. They are all views of the same state, so a tool or a colour chosen on
one applies everywhere. The toolbar strip cannot start a drag on any screen,
though dragging upward into it still works.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xclip (1),
.BR wl\-copy (1)
